WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.721
A dependable loop needs explicit state.

2
00:00:02.721 --> 00:00:05.896
A growing conversation transcript is not enough.

3
00:00:05.896 --> 00:00:10.884
Transcripts are useful records, but they are not structured control data.

4
00:00:10.884 --> 00:00:16.326
They can bury the current objective, mix stale assumptions with fresh evidence,

5
00:00:16.326 --> 00:00:19.047
and make recovery difficult after interruption.

6
00:00:19.047 --> 00:00:26.304
State is the application-maintained record that tells the loop where it is and what has happened.

7
00:00:26.304 --> 00:00:31.746
It is the part operators can query without rereading every generated sentence.

8
00:00:31.746 --> 00:00:38.548
Useful state fields include a task identifier, current status, current objective or subtask, completed actions,

9
00:00:38.548 --> 00:00:44.898
pending actions, evidence collected, files or records changed, errors encountered, retry counts, budget consumed,

10
00:00:44.898 --> 00:00:48.526
approval status, last meaningful progress, and terminal reason.

11
00:00:48.526 --> 00:00:52.607
These fields do not all belong in every prompt.

12
00:00:52.607 --> 00:00:58.956
They belong in the harness so the application can reason about progress and safety.

13
00:00:58.956 --> 00:01:01.224
State is different from context.

14
00:01:01.224 --> 00:01:04.852
State is the structured record the application maintains.

15
00:01:04.852 --> 00:01:10.748
Context is the selected information supplied to the model for a particular iteration.

16
00:01:10.748 --> 00:01:18.457
A good loop selects the minimum useful context from state, source artifacts, recent observations, and stable policy.

17
00:01:18.457 --> 00:01:27.074
Copying the entire state and history into every call is expensive and can make important facts harder to find.

18
00:01:27.074 --> 00:01:32.063
Selective context also keeps obsolete attempts from competing with current evidence.

19
00:01:32.063 --> 00:01:34.784
The control flow should be explicit.

20
00:01:34.784 --> 00:01:37.958
Load the task contract and current state.

21
00:01:37.958 --> 00:01:39.319
Construct focused context.

22
00:01:39.319 --> 00:01:41.586
Request one proposed next action.

23
00:01:41.586 --> 00:01:44.761
Validate that proposal against schema and policy.

24
00:01:44.761 --> 00:01:46.575
Execute one approved action.

25
00:01:46.575 --> 00:01:50.657
Normalize and record the observation. Evaluate progress. Update state.

26
00:01:50.657 --> 00:01:53.378
Then continue, pause, escalate, or terminate.

27
00:01:53.378 --> 00:01:59.727
Each iteration should produce a defined state transition, not just another paragraph of output.

28
00:01:59.727 --> 00:02:02.448
Durable state supports resumability and governance.

29
00:02:02.448 --> 00:02:08.797
If a coding loop pauses after editing two files and running one failing test,

30
00:02:08.797 --> 00:02:16.507
it should resume from a checkpoint without repeating every earlier action or assuming external conditions stayed fixed.

31
00:02:16.507 --> 00:02:23.309
Engineers should be able to see the test command, failing tests, edited files, current hypothesis,

32
00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:30.112
previous repair attempt, remaining budget, and stop reason without reconstructing the run from free-form text.
